WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.016 --> 00:00:18.671
"Grandpa! I got a starring role in the class play!"

2
00:00:19.973 --> 00:00:22.095
Laurie bounded into Grandpa's study.

3
00:00:22.115 --> 00:00:24.136
"That's terrific!"

4
00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:26.198
Grandpa said, giving her a hug.

5
00:00:26.218 --> 00:00:27.720
"Tell me more."

6
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:34.702
"The title of the play is Medieval Nights, but our teacher says it's based on some book called

7
00:00:34.762 --> 00:00:36.162
The Canterbury Tales."

8
00:00:37.682 --> 00:00:42.004
"Ah! That's one of the most famous books of the Middle Ages.

9
00:00:43.084 --> 00:00:47.305
It was written by a man named Geoffrey Chaucer," Grandpa said.

10
00:00:47.325 --> 00:00:51.926
"The play is about a group of travelers going to visit a cathedral.

11
00:00:53.127 --> 00:00:57.688
I'm an English queen who meets a knight and sends him on a long journey!"

12
00:00:59.116 --> 00:01:00.137
Grandpa smiled.

13
00:01:00.157 --> 00:01:04.159
"Then I'm sure you live in a magnificent castle!

14
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.422
How much do you know about cathedrals, knights, and castles?"

15
00:01:08.883 --> 00:01:09.423
he asked.

16
00:01:10.784 --> 00:01:12.885
"Not much," Laurie admitted.

17
00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:16.708
"My class is going to study the Middle Ages next week."

18
00:01:16.728 --> 00:01:20.850
"I could give you an introduction," Grandpa offered.

19
00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:26.794
"Sure." Laurie settled down on the couch.

20
00:01:26.814 --> 00:01:34.326
"The Middle Ages refers to the time between the collapse of the Roman Empire and the beginning of

21
00:01:34.366 --> 00:01:40.308
the European Renaissance, so from around the fifth century to the fifteenth century.

22
00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:46.330
In Europe the Christian Church had become the richest, most powerful institution.

23
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:53.472
You, Queen Laurie, might have been baptized in a cathedral, like the one in your play.

24
00:01:54.636 --> 00:02:01.080
You'd attend church every Sunday, get married in a church, pay taxes to the church, and go there

25
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.021
to seek advice and make confessions.

26
00:02:04.341 --> 00:02:08.083
Church buildings became really beautiful during the Middle Ages.

27
00:02:09.164 --> 00:02:15.828
Medieval architects designed tall buildings with pointy steeples that reached toward heaven.

28
00:02:17.529 --> 00:02:23.352
The insides were filled with expensive sculptures, golden goblets, and other fancy things.

29
00:02:24.508 --> 00:02:28.893
Huge, stained-glass windows flooded the church with colored light.

30
00:02:30.074 --> 00:02:35.619
But these beautiful windows weren't just decoration—they also told stories.

31
00:02:38.102 --> 00:02:43.868
Many people those days couldn't read, so how could they learn the stories of the Bible?

32
00:02:44.909 --> 00:02:47.732
Well, priests could tell them of course.

33
00:02:48.812 --> 00:02:54.875
But people could also look up at the massive windows and see the story of Jesus, for example,

34
00:02:55.296 --> 00:02:56.416
pictured in the glass."

35
00:02:56.436 --> 00:02:59.738
"Wow Like a storybook."

36
00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:06.201
Laurie gazed at the windows in the study, trying to imagine a story painted there.

37
00:03:06.221 --> 00:03:12.125
"I bet there is a monk and a nun in your play too," Grandpa said.

38
00:03:12.145 --> 00:03:18.308
"Yep. But I'm glad I get to be the queen and wear a nice costume," Laurie confessed.

39
00:03:19.284 --> 00:03:23.007
"The nun just wears a boring gown and something to cover her head."

40
00:03:24.208 --> 00:03:29.153
"Men and women who became monks and nuns devoted their lives to the church.

41
00:03:30.394 --> 00:03:33.697
They didn't have time to worry about things like pretty clothes.

42
00:03:35.258 --> 00:03:38.841
They lived in their own communities, separated from the town.

43
00:03:39.962 --> 00:03:46.748
Monks wore plain, brown robes, ate simple food, and spent their days praying and studying the Bible.

44
00:03:47.776 --> 00:03:50.698
They were often well-educated and could read and write.

45
00:03:51.859 --> 00:03:55.982
Many copied out long sections of the Bible in beautiful script.

46
00:03:57.142 --> 00:04:03.567
Nuns devoted most of their day to prayer too, but they were busy with chores such as cooking,

47
00:04:03.727 --> 00:04:04.848
cleaning, and sewing.

48
00:04:06.549 --> 00:04:11.212
Many medieval Christians wanted to create an entirely Christian world.

49
00:04:11.912 --> 00:04:15.195
But there were still strong empires with different religions.

50
00:04:16.195 --> 00:04:22.137
For example, the Muslim Abbasid Empire ruled northern Africa and the Middle East.

51
00:04:23.418 --> 00:04:31.826
European Christians didn't like that their holy land—including Jerusalem, where Christianity developed—was controlled by Muslims.

52
00:04:32.927 --> 00:04:36.030
Many Christians journeyed to these places to worship.

53
00:04:37.531 --> 00:04:42.396
And church leaders worried about Christians' safety because sometimes they were attacked.

54
00:04:43.432 --> 00:04:50.115
So the pope asked barons—wealthy landowners— to gather their armies and fight for Christianity.

55
00:04:51.055 --> 00:04:59.358
These battles, known as the Crusades, lasted for nearly two hundred years and killed almost nine million people.

56
00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:05.541
The Christian fighters were called Crusaders and wore red crosses on their armor.

57
00:05:07.402 --> 00:05:08.322
Many were knights.

58
00:05:09.502 --> 00:05:11.883
Is the knight in your play a Crusader, Laurie?"

59
00:05:12.144 --> 00:05:13.124
Grandpa asked.

60
00:05:14.572 --> 00:05:15.272
"I don't know!

61
00:05:16.393 --> 00:05:21.414
All I know is that he travels around and when he finally returns, he gets married and lives

62
00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:23.094
in a castle," Laurie said.

63
00:05:23.115 --> 00:05:26.636
"Is he chivalrous and handsome?"

64
00:05:26.976 --> 00:05:28.356
Grandpa asked, smiling.

65
00:05:28.376 --> 00:05:32.497
"Handsome? No!

66
00:05:32.717 --> 00:05:35.518
Yuck," Laurie answered, crinkling her nose.

67
00:05:35.538 --> 00:05:38.859
"And I don't know what chivalrous means."

68
00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.846
"Chivalrous means good, honorable, and polite to women.

69
00:05:45.947 --> 00:05:48.449
All knights were expected to be chivalrous.

70
00:05:49.509 --> 00:05:53.452
They practiced this, along with fighting skills, from a young age.

71
00:05:54.593 --> 00:05:56.314
Knights worked for barons.

72
00:05:57.335 --> 00:06:03.720
In exchange for protection, barons gave their knights some land and maybe a nice castle to live in.

73
00:06:04.761 --> 00:06:09.284
Battles were often deadly, so knights were respected for their bravery.

74
00:06:10.336 --> 00:06:14.459
Plus they made an awe-inspiring sight when they dressed for battle.

75
00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:22.125
In the early Middle Ages, a knight's armor was a suit made of tiny iron rings linked together.

76
00:06:23.146 --> 00:06:30.691
But eventually knights realized they needed more protection and began wearing suits made of iron and steel plates.

77
00:06:31.872 --> 00:06:36.215
It was like having a full-body shield that weighed over 22 kilograms!"

78
00:06:38.524 --> 00:06:41.465
"I'd rather hear about the castles," Laurie requested.

79
00:06:41.485 --> 00:06:44.006
"Since I'll be living in one."

80
00:06:44.026 --> 00:06:46.107
"Of course.

81
00:06:47.228 --> 00:06:52.210
Castles were large, sturdy stone buildings with many rooms.

82
00:06:53.270 --> 00:07:00.293
There were great candlelit halls for feasts and celebrations, but also dark, damp prisons.

83
00:07:01.393 --> 00:07:07.176
Castles had to protect the owner, as well as the servants, guards, and entertainers who lived there.

84
00:07:08.384 --> 00:07:11.467
Neighboring noblemen often fought each other for land.

85
00:07:12.408 --> 00:07:17.692
So many castles had stone defense walls that circled the entire castle.

86
00:07:19.294 --> 00:07:21.716
Sometimes there were even two or three walls.

87
00:07:22.777 --> 00:07:28.361
Guards would stand on top of the walls and shoot arrows or throw huge rocks at intruders.

88
00:07:29.502 --> 00:07:35.868
Many castles also had a moat, which was a man-made canal around the castle.

89
00:07:36.884 --> 00:07:42.449
In order to cross it, visitors needed to have a bridge let down, or else swim across.

90
00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:47.793
The tall towers were often lookout points to spot intruders.

91
00:07:48.954 --> 00:07:51.696
Or perhaps to spot a knight returning from his journey!"

92
00:07:53.257 --> 00:07:55.779
"Laurie! Will you help me make dinner?"

93
00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:58.201
Laurie's mom called from downstairs.

94
00:07:58.221 --> 00:08:00.103
"Coming, Mom!"

95
00:08:00.443 --> 00:08:01.464
Laurie hollered back.

96
00:08:02.625 --> 00:08:03.525
Then she giggled.

97
00:08:03.545 --> 00:08:06.448
"Silly Mom.

98
00:08:07.452 --> 00:08:08.954
Queens don't cook dinner!"

99
00:08:10.035 --> 00:08:12.838
She turned to Grandpa and raised her chin like a queen.

100
00:08:12.858 --> 00:08:17.964
"Be sure to join us for our feast in the great hall tonight."

101
00:08:19.045 --> 00:08:23.751
Bowing slightly, Grandpa answered, "It would be my pleasure, Your Highness."
